Bluetooth audio
===============

Rockbox can play to Bluetooth headphones and speakers on the Aigo Eros Q
family. This file covers setting it up, using it, and adding Bluetooth
status to a theme. The manual has the same settings in more detail
(Settings > Bluetooth Settings).


Which players
-------------

Only hardware revision 4, the ones that use the "erosq_2024" update file:

  Aigo Eros Q    V2.2
  Hifiwalker H2  V1.9 to V2.3
  Surfans F20    V3.4

The Bluetooth chip is a Cypress CYW43438 on UART0. Older revisions wire the
chip differently and Bluetooth will refuse to start on them.


Installing
----------

Bluetooth needs no extra files. Install the erosqnative build the usual way
by extracting the zip to the root of the SD card. The chip firmware is built
into rockbox.erosq.


First connection
----------------

1. Put the headphones in pairing mode.
2. Settings > Bluetooth Settings > Devices.
3. Wait a few seconds for them to show up, select them and press Play.

The list title says what is happening (connecting, paired, connected). In
the list:

  Play             connect to the selected device
  Long press Menu  disconnect it
  Menu             turn Bluetooth off
  Back             leave the list, Bluetooth stays on

"Scan again" at the top of the list searches again. Only audio devices are
listed.

Cars and some speakers ask for a code when pairing:

  Both show a number   the player asks if it matches, press Play for yes
  Car asks you to type the player shows the number to type
  Old style PIN        type it in, it's often 0000 or 1234

If pairing fails, just try again. The player throws away the half finished
pairing so the next attempt starts clean.

Headphones that are busy with a phone can be slow to answer. The player
tries three times, which is usually enough to take them over.

After the first time, turning Bluetooth on reconnects the last headphones
by itself, and most earbuds reconnect when you take them out of the case.


What works
----------

- Play, pause, next and previous from the headphone buttons
- In-ear detection: taking the earbuds out pauses, putting them back resumes
- Playback pauses when the headphones disconnect or go out of range
- Volume sync: if the headphones support it, the player's volume keys set
  the headphones' volume and the headphones' buttons move Rockbox's volume
- Headphone battery level, shown in the Devices list and in themes
- Title, artist and album for car stereos and other displays
- Codecs: SBC, aptX, aptX HD and LDAC. SBC is the one that has seen real
  use; the others should work but have had little testing.

Audio always goes out at 44.1 kHz and Rockbox resamples anything else.


Settings
--------

Bluetooth            on/off. Can be put on the Quick Screen and in
                     Shortcuts, which is the quickest way to get to it.
Devices              the list above
Enable on Startup    turn on at boot and reconnect (default off)
Auto Off When Idle   turn off after this long without a connection
                     (default 10 minutes, Off to never turn off)
Preferred Codec      Auto, LDAC, aptX HD, aptX or SBC
LDAC Quality         Adaptive, 990, 660 or 330 kbps
Show Bluetooth Icon  status bar and theme icons
Forget Devices       unpair a device
Debug Logging        write bt_log.txt and bt_hci.pcap (default off)

To change the name headphones see, put it on the first line of
/.rockbox/playername.txt. Without that file the name is "Rockbox".

Paired devices are stored in /.rockbox/bt_config.conf, including their
link keys, so don't share that file.


If something goes wrong
-----------------------

Turn on Debug Logging and do the thing that fails again. Rockbox then writes:

  /.rockbox/bt_log.txt   what the stack and Rockbox did, with timestamps
  /.rockbox/bt_hci.pcap  all traffic with the Bluetooth chip during
                         connection setup and the first seconds of
                         playback; open it with Wireshark

"Bluetooth failed to start (controller ...)" means the chip didn't answer.
"Could not connect" usually means the headphones are busy with another
device, like a phone. Dropouts mean the radio link is struggling; a lower
LDAC quality, or SBC, helps.


Themes
======

Rockbox's own status bar shows a small Bluetooth icon by itself: dashed while
Bluetooth is on, solid once headphones are connected. Themes with their own
status bar (an .sbs file) need two tags to do the same.


The tags
--------

%Bt  Bluetooth state, for conditionals:

       %?Bt<off|on|connected|playing>

     "on" means Bluetooth is on but nothing is connected. "playing" means
     audio is going out over Bluetooth right now.

%Bb  Headphone battery, 0 to 100. Behaves like %bl:

       %?Bb<unknown|0-19|20-39|...|100>

     The first option is used when nothing is connected or the headphones
     haven't reported a level yet. The other options split 0 to 100
     evenly and the last one is only used at exactly 100. Used outside a
     conditional it prints the number, or nothing when unknown.

Both read as off and unknown when Show Bluetooth Icon is turned off, so a
theme doesn't have to check that setting.


Adding them to a theme
----------------------

1. Draw the icons. Rockbox images are BMP files. The usual way to do states
   is a strip of equal sized frames stacked on top of each other, like the
   battery and repeat icons most themes already have. For the Bluetooth
   icon three or four frames work well:

     frame 1  on, nothing connected (a dotted or faded rune is common)
     frame 2  connected
     frame 3  playing (optional, can be the same as connected)

   For the headphone battery, four frames from one bar to full is enough.
   1-bit (black and white) BMPs are drawn in the theme's foreground colour,
   so they follow colour changes for free. Keep them the same height as the
   icons next to them.

2. Load them near the top of the .sbs file, with the number of frames as
   the last argument:

     %xl(bt,bluetooth.bmp,0,0,3)
     %xl(hpbatt,hp_battery.bmp,0,0,4)

3. Give each one its own viewport and pick a frame with a conditional.
   An empty first option hides the icon:

     # Bluetooth
     %V(95,8,20,20,-)
     %?Bt<|%xd(bt,1)|%xd(bt,2)|%xd(bt,3)>

     # Headphone battery
     %V(118,8,10,20,-)
     %?Bb<|%xd(hpbatt,1)|%xd(hpbatt,2)|%xd(hpbatt,3)|%xd(hpbatt,4)|%xd(hpbatt,4)>

   With six options in %?Bb<...> that is: hidden, then 0-24, 25-49, 50-74,
   75-99 and 100 percent. Repeating the last frame for 75-99 and 100 means
   "full" shows from 75 percent up. Use more or fewer options to change the
   steps.

4. If the theme has its own Now Playing screen (.wps), add the same lines
   there too. The .sbs is not shown on that screen.

5. Check the files before copying them to the player. checkwps (build it
   with tools/configure, type C) points at the line with any mistake:

     checkwps.erosqnative .rockbox/wps/mytheme.sbs

   The theme editor in utils/themeeditor can preview both tags; set
   "Bluetooth State" and "Headphone Battery" in its device settings.

Things to watch for:

- Viewports that overlap clear each other. Put the icon where nothing else
  draws, or put its viewport after the other one in the file.
- Leave room for the longest text next to the icons, for example a 12 hour
  clock ("12:34 PM") is a lot wider than "16:34".
- Players without Bluetooth just read the tags as off, so the same theme
  works there. Rockbox versions from before these tags existed will refuse
  to load it though, so mention it if you share the theme.
